"error boundary" is a notebook page
	<header level="1">
		ErrorBoundary
		<header>Shows an error in place of what it guards, when that throws.</>
	</>

	<text class="lede" paragraph>A guard around one of the editor's panes. When drawing what's inside it goes wrong, the pane shows the error instead, and the rest of the page keeps working.</>
	<list class="spell-meta">
		<item icon="user">Written for: anyone writing or changing a pane of the spell app</>
		<item icon="image">A picture, not live: it draws nothing of its own until something throws, then an <link target="src-packages-app-docs-errornotice-html" link="/error-notice">ErrorDisplay</></>
	</>

	<section label="1. What it looks like">
		<icon slot="icon" name="image"/>
		<text paragraph>It looks like nothing while all is well. When what it guards throws, its default stand-in is an <link target="src-packages-app-docs-errornotice-html" link="/error-notice">ErrorDisplay</> of the error, which looks like this, in the pane rather than over the page:</>
		<image src="/components/app/_images/ErrorNotice.png" alt="A red error message with a header, Error, and the error's text" appearance="bordered rounded"/>
		<text class="meta" paragraph>A picture, taken by <text code>yarn site:pictures</> in <link target="src-spell-app" link="/"><text code>spell-app</></>.</>
	</>

	<section label="2. Using it">
		<icon slot="icon" name="code"/>
		<text paragraph>Each viewer wraps what it draws in one. The input editor falls back to a plain-text editor, and tells the editor of the error:</>
		<text paragraph>InputEditor.tsx · the code editor, guarded:</>
		<markdown>
			```typescript
			<div class="InputEditor">
			  <ErrorBoundary
			    onError={(error) => props.showError?.(error)}
			    fallback={() => <FallbackEditor />}
			  >
			    <FileInputEditor />
			  </ErrorBoundary>
			</div>
			```
		</>
		<list bulleted>
			<item>WHY every pane wants one: an error nobody catches stops Solid updating the WHOLE page, not just the pane.</>
			<item>It's Solid's <text code>&lt;Errored&gt;</>, with the app's defaults: an ErrorDisplay of the error, unless you pass <text code>fallback</>.</>
			<item>It heals by itself: when the data underneath changes and its children draw, they come back. <text code>fallback</> is also given <text code>reset()</>, to try again now.</>
			<item><text code>onError</> hears of each error caught, to log it or show it with <text code>editor.showError()</>. It may change the editor's state: it runs outside the drawing.</>
			<item>A thrown value that isn't an <text code>Error</> is made into one.</>
		</>
	</>

	<section label="3. Props, and where it's drawn">
		<icon slot="icon" name="list check"/>
		<html>
			<ui-docs-api family="ErrorBoundary"></>
		</>
		<text class="meta" paragraph>From its code, by <text code>yarn site:data</> in <link target="src-spell-app" link="/"><text code>spell-app</></>.</>
	</>

